meta {
  name: Set Sending Domain
  type: http
  seq: 14
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/agency/email/sending-domain
  body: json
  auth: bearer
}

auth:bearer {
  token: {{ownerAccessToken}}
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/json
}

body:json {
  {
    "domain": "texasholdings.com"
  }
}

tests {
  test("is unavailable without a provider key", function () {
    // Local development has no RESEND_API_KEY, so `UnavailableEmailProvider`
    // refuses. Refusing is correct: a stub that returned "verified" would make
    // the worker build a `From:` on an unverified domain, and the first real
    // send would fail non-retriably.
    expect([201, 503]).to.include(res.getStatus());
  });

  test("never lands verified", function () {
    if (res.getStatus() === 201) {
      expect(res.getBody().sendingStatus).to.equal("pending");
    }
  });
}

docs {
  # POST /agency/email/sending-domain

  Register a domain with the email provider (Resend) and return the DNS records
  the agency has to publish.

  **Module** — none. **Permission** — `agency:email:write`.

  **Body** — `{ "domain": "texasholdings.com" }`. Normalised server-side.

  ## This is a different domain from `agency/domains`
  Deliberately separate, and they verify independently:
  - an **app** hostname needs a CNAME/A record at the edge, verified by us;
  - a **sending** domain needs DKIM/SPF/DMARC at the registrar, verified by the
    provider.

  An agency may well use `texasholdings.com` for both. That is two DNS record
  sets doing two jobs.

  ## Always lands `pending`
  `SenderIdentityService` keys off `sendingStatus` to decide whether it may send
  from the domain, and an optimistic `verified` here does not delay an email —
  it **loses** it. Resend rejects an unverified `From:` with
  `invalid_from_address`, which `ResendTransport` treats as non-retriable. Until
  verification passes, mail keeps going out from the platform address, which is
  correct behaviour rather than a degradation.

  Registering a second domain replaces the first, including at the provider —
  two live records would leave no way to say which one `fromLocalPart` belongs
  to.

  **Response `201`** — the **Get Email Settings** shape with `sendingStatus:
  "pending"` and `dnsRecords` populated (SPF `MX` + `TXT`, DKIM `CNAME`/`TXT`,
  tracking `CNAME`). The records are cached on the agency so a page reload
  between attempts still shows them.

  **Errors**
  - `400` — malformed domain.
  - `403` — caller lacks `agency:email:write`.
  - `409` — that domain is already verified for this agency.
  - `503` — `RESEND_API_KEY` is not configured, or the provider rejected the
    request. Email keeps sending from the platform address.
}
